WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.490
"Hardworking people are up against a small
group that holds far too much power.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.080
Not just in our economy.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.460
But also in our Democracy."

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:13.700
Elizabeth Warren likes to say she's a champion of the little guy.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:17.770
But she recently announced a major policy
proposal that would provide more than a trillion dollars

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:22.120
in aid that would disproportionately
benefit the relatively well off and well connected.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:31.910
"We are going to roll back student loan debt
for about 95% of students who have debt."

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:36.900
The idea is to eliminate debt up to
$50,000 for people with household incomes

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.000
under $100,000.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.000
And more limited debt cancellation

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.870
for households making between $100,000 and $250,000 a year.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:48.400
By her own estimates, the full plan, which
also includes funds for Pell Grants and historically black colleges,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.609
would cost about $1.25 trillion.

00:00:51.609 --> 00:00:54.692
"This is about opportunity for everyone."

00:00:54.692 --> 00:00:58.199
But the nature of college attendance and student
loans means that Warren's loan forgiveness plan

00:00:58.199 --> 00:01:02.139
is actually a massive giveaway to the
relatively well-off.

00:01:02.139 --> 00:01:06.400
Only about a third of people over 25 have
a college degree, making them a comparatively elite group,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.000
whose elite status is reinforced
by, among other things,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.250
the connections they make while at college.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.330
College graduates earn about $1 million more
during their lifetimes than non-college graduates,

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.340
according to a Georgetown University study.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:23.680
A separate study from Pew found college graduates
typically earn about $17,500 more annually

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:26.040
than people who only had high school degrees.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.340
College graduates aren't, for the most part,
super rich.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.600
But generally speaking, they are far more
comfortable than the majority

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:32.700
who lack such degrees.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.119
And while many of the people who would benefit
from the plan currently have modest incomes,

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:38.400
that's partly because many of them are young people

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.400
with relatively high future earnings potential.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.479
This is a plan that would spend taxpayer money
to benefit them.

00:01:43.479 --> 00:01:46.100
Warren's defenders might respond that it's still a downward transfer,

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.200
since the whole thing will be paid for by a new tax on the super wealthy.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.399
There are, however, a few problems with using
a wealth tax as a financing mechanism.

00:01:53.399 --> 00:01:56.280
The first is that the tax might not be constitutional.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.649
Even if it is, another problem is that it's
likely to raise far less money than projected.

00:02:00.649 --> 00:02:03.600
That's why most countries that have tried
wealth taxes over the last two decades

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.600
have abandoned them.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.800
The third, as the The Washington Examiner's
Philip Klein points out that, is that

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.150
Warren has also suggested that this same wealth tax could be used to help pay for a whole

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.760
slew of other progressive policy ideas.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.500
Those include Medicare for All, the Green
New Deal, and subsidized child care, which,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.010
all together, would cost tens of trillions
of dollars—far more than even the most generous

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:24.450
wealth tax would raise.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:28.900
"I'm tired of a Washington that works for
the rich and the powerful.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.500
I want a Washington that works for the rest
of America.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.920
That's why I'm in this fight."

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.790
Ultimately, what Warren wants to do is tax
the wealthy to help the merely well off,

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.709
rather than prioritize programs that help the truly needy.

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:45.300
Warren isn't helping the little guy—she's
helping the folks already on top stay that way.

